# Header

A header identifies the service and gives consistent access to its main destinations.

These are civica reference implementations for component authors. They do not replace native Home Assistant behaviour or demonstrate a connection to a live home.

## Identity and navigation have separate responsibilities

This page owns the household header and optional identity contract. Use the [generic-header variant](generic-header.html) for a focused identity and home link. Add [service navigation](service-navigation.html) only when the page needs several stable destinations; that component owns current-route and small-screen menu behaviour.

A clear household or business name is the default. A crest or logo is optional; see [Personalise your home](../get-started/personalisation.html). The project logo belongs to project documentation and promotion. All variants use the permitted local font stack. A source reference to a government or NHS header does not authorise its identity assets. A documentation header is not proof that a native login, account screen or onboarding flow has been redesigned. Preserve Home Assistant’s actual authentication and routing boundaries.

## When to use it

Use a minimal identity header for a focused journey and add only the navigation, search or account information that users need. Keep the service name visible.

## When not to use it

Do not reproduce NHS branding or add a second application header inside Home Assistant views. A theme companion adapts supported native shell elements; it does not replace routing or authentication.

## Home Assistant adaptation

The NHS header’s identity rules apply to NHS services, not this project. Use the household’s chosen name and an optional authorised image on a readable surface. Preserve Home Assistant’s native menu, account actions and permissions. A constrained task may need fewer exits than a browsing dashboard.

## Accessibility and behaviour

Use a header landmark, an appropriately named home link and labelled navigation. Keep text and icons visible on every masthead colour, including yellow focus. Expose current navigation state without relying solely on an underline or colour.

